<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What a signed Present is allowed to do.

  A signed build runs under the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime
  will only load libraries signed by the same team as the app. NDI's runtime
  is signed by NDI (team W8U66ET244) and Present by us, so without this the
  bundled `libndi.dylib` is refused by the loader and every signed build
  reports "NDI runtime unavailable" — the library is right there in
  `Contents/Resources/ndi`, and macOS will not open it.

  This is the entitlement Apple provides for exactly that case: an app that
  ships a third party's signed library. It does not disable the signature
  check on our own code, and it does not weaken the runtime in any other way.

  The microphone is the same story. `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` in
  `Info.plist` is what the operator is shown when macOS asks, but under the
  hardened runtime the ask never happens without this entitlement: the app is
  refused before anyone sees a prompt, and the scripture agent reports the
  microphone blocked with nothing the operator can do about it. An unsigned
  build has no hardened runtime to enforce either of these, which is why both
  only appear once the app is signed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Capture devices, for the screen composer and HDMI boxes. Same rule as
	     the microphone: the hardened runtime refuses them without this. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
